Looking at this problem, I need to count squares (side 1/5, half-side 1/10) and circles (radius 1/10) centered at integer lattice points that the segment from (0,0) to (1001,429) intersects.

## Setup

The line through (0,0) and (1001,429) has equation 3x − 7y = 0 (since gcd(1001,429) = 143, giving primitive direction (7,3)). The segment is parameterized as (x, (3/7)x) for x ∈ [0, 1001].

## Circles (n)

The perpendicular distance from lattice point (a,b) to the line is |3a − 7b|/√58. For this to be ≤ 1/10, we need |3a − 7b| ≤ √58/10 ≈ 0.762. Since 3a − 7b is an integer, this forces **3a − 7b = 0**, giving (a,b) = (7k, 3k).

For points off the line, the minimum perpendicular distance is 1/√58 ≈ 0.131 > 0.1. Near endpoints, the distance to (0,0) or (1001,429) is ≥ 1 for any other lattice point. So only on-line points qualify.

The projection parameter is t = k/143, requiring k ∈ {0, 1, ..., 143}.

**n = 144**

## Squares (m)

The square at (a,b) is [a−0.1, a+0.1] × [b−0.1, b+0.1]. The segment intersects it iff there exists x ∈ [0,1001] with x ∈ [a−0.1, a+0.1] and (3/7)x ∈ [b−0.1, b+0.1], i.e., x ∈ [(7/3)(b−0.1), (7/3)(b+0.1)].

